From 83aefebdf0f1801ac0dcadffd5fc54b3836eb0be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 05:52:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] microblaze: keep the dynamic area's accesses before sp is
 reset

When a frame pointer is used, the epilogue sets sp from it, which
releases the area that alloca, VLAs and -fstack-check's dynamic
allocations use.  Nothing kept the scheduler from moving the last loads
and stores of that area after the move, where an interrupt, or anything
else pushing onto the stack, can overwrite them first.

This epilogue comes from the GCC 3 MIPS one, which emitted a blockage
right before the same move; the copy left it out.  MIPS itself lost
that blockage when its epilogue was rewritten for GCC 3.4, put a
barrier back with r179432 (d9b59f5634a6, "mips.c (mips_frame_barrier):
New function."), and, because that barrier was only a clobber of the
frame and did not stop accesses through a copy of sp, made it a full
blockage with 61db64a590e5 ("MIPS: Frame barrier fix").

Emit a blockage before setting sp from the frame pointer.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

gcc/ChangeLog:

	* config/microblaze/microblaze.cc (microblaze_expand_epilogue):
	Emit a blockage before setting sp from the frame pointer.

gcc/testsuite/ChangeLog:

	* gcc.target/microblaze/others/epilogue-alloca.c: New test.
---
 gcc/config/microblaze/microblaze.cc           | 11 +++++++++--
 .../microblaze/others/epilogue-alloca.c       | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 28 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/microblaze/others/epilogue-alloca.c

diff --git a/gcc/config/microblaze/microblaze.cc b/gcc/config/microblaze/microblaze.cc
index 2cb393676..f1d81df8f 100644
--- a/gcc/config/microblaze/microblaze.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/microblaze/microblaze.cc
@@ -3250,9 +3250,16 @@ microblaze_expand_epilogue (void)
 	 sub-routine return address only from the current stack top and not
 	 from the frame pointer (which we restore below). (frame_pointer + 0)
 	 might have been over-written since alloca allocates memory on the
-	 current stack.  */
+	 current stack.
+
+	 Setting sp from the frame pointer frees the dynamic area (alloca,
+	 VLAs), so keep every access to it before that: anything pushed on
+	 this stack, such as an interrupt frame, may overwrite it after.  */
       if (frame_pointer_needed)
-	emit_insn (gen_movsi (stack_pointer_rtx, hard_frame_pointer_rtx));
+	{
+	  emit_insn (gen_blockage ());
+	  emit_insn (gen_movsi (stack_pointer_rtx, hard_frame_pointer_rtx));
+	}
 
       /* _restore_ registers for epilogue.  */
       save_restore_insns (0);
diff --git a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/microblaze/others/epilogue-alloca.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/microblaze/others/epilogue-alloca.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..e932aacdd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/microblaze/others/epilogue-alloca.c
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+/* { dg-do compile } */
+
+/* The epilogue sets sp from the frame pointer, which frees the alloca
+   area, and nothing stopped the scheduler from moving the last accesses
+   to that area after it.  An interrupt arriving in between would
+   overwrite them.  */
+
+void g (char *);
+
+int
+f (int n)
+{
+  char *p = __builtin_alloca (n);
+  g (p);
+  return p[0] + p[n - 1];
+}
+
+/* { dg-final { scan-assembler "addk\tr1,r19,r0" } } */
+/* { dg-final { scan-assembler-not "addk\tr1,r19,r0(\n\t\[^\n\]*)*\n\tlbui?\t" } } */
